WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.800
A long time ago, in a galaxy far, far&nbsp;
away, “Return of the Jedi” had yet to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.720
released. What did people think was going&nbsp;
to happen? What was spoiler culture like?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.080
And how did critics describe Jabba the&nbsp;
Hutt to people who hadn’t seen him yet?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:16.000
Here’s what it was really like to&nbsp;
see “Return Of The Jedi” in 1983.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.200
Some movie series open to mediocre&nbsp;
reception and find their legacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.960
years later as cult classics. That&nbsp;
was not the case with "Star Wars."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.880
The first two films had become cultural&nbsp;
phenomenons and audiences eagerly awaited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.960
"Return of the Jedi," the final installment&nbsp;
of the original trilogy. Think back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.960
excitement in the air on opening night of&nbsp;
"Spider-Man: No Way Home," another threequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.520
or even "The Rise of Skywalker," the third of the&nbsp;
"Star Wars" sequel trilogy films. This isn't a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:45.280
new thing. Audiences in 1983 did the same thing&nbsp;
we do in 2022: totally geek out at the movies.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:51.760
[Star Wars noises]

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.760
Long before electronic seat reservations&nbsp;
were standard — or even fathomable — fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.120
in Los Angeles waited outside their&nbsp;
theater for days prior to the midnight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.800
debut of "Return of the Jedi" on May&nbsp;
25, 1983. And by days, we mean days.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.440
“We’ve been here for six days, and it’s great!”

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.440
And why wouldn't they? After all, the&nbsp;
previous film "The Empire Strikes Back"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.720
had left everyone with the cliffhanger&nbsp;
that Darth Vader was Luke's father.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.600
What could possibly follow THAT revelation? In&nbsp;
1983, the Denver Post claimed that "Return of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.400
the Jedi" was, quote, "one of the most anticipated&nbsp;
movies of all time," sharing a photo of moviegoers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.040
waiting outside in costume as their favorite&nbsp;
characters. Adoration for this galaxy far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.080
far away has truly been bringing&nbsp;
people together for generations.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.880
One of the most satisfying things about being&nbsp;
a devoted fan of something — whether it be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.960
show or a book series or, in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.200
Star Wars — is being in on all of the inside&nbsp;
jokes and lesser known deets. These days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.520
the sheer amount of stuff to geek out on is&nbsp;
endless. But back in 1983, possessing deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.160
knowledge about any given pop culture phenomenon&nbsp;
was a little more novel of an experience.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.280
That being said, if you were there on&nbsp;
opening night of “Return of the Jedi”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.480
you would have seen numerous in-the-know&nbsp;
fans wearing shirts bearing the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:02.720
title of the trilogy’s epic conclusion.&nbsp;
Yep, you heard us right! As it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.320
“Return of the Jedi” was originally&nbsp;
titled “Revenge of the Jedi”.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:08.400
“Oh, I’m sorry. Did I break your concentration?”

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.400
And this wasn’t just an internal, temporary&nbsp;
name that was changed before announcing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.840
movie to the public, either. It made it&nbsp;
so far in the post-production process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.240
that the first trailer for the movie advertised&nbsp;
"Revenge of the Jedi" to prospective audiences!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.040
Eventually, "Revenge" was swapped for "Return”&nbsp;
— and the rest is, of course, movie history.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.040
A good idea never really goes away, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.800
because in 2005 the removed word would&nbsp;
finally make its way into a "Star Wars"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.840
movie with the release of "Star Wars:&nbsp;
Episode III – Revenge of the Sith.”

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.000
“Gooooood!”

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.240
We've all been there. You innocently log&nbsp;
into Twitter around 8am and start scrolling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:45.360
only to find that some guy just had&nbsp;
to be the first one to drop spoilers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.480
about the new episode of "The Mandalorian"&nbsp;
that dropped in the middle of the night.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:50.400
“What else did he say?”

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.440
Or maybe a headline unashamedly gives away&nbsp;
the end credits scene of "Eternals." Do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:03.840
get mad when someone spoils a movie or television&nbsp;
show before you've gotten the chance to see it?

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.360
“You shut up! Shut up! You&nbsp;
shut up! Shut up! Shut up!”

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.560
Dodging spoilers is an art, but it's not a new&nbsp;
practice. While 1983 audiences may not have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:17.280
avoiding tweets, they had means of their own to&nbsp;
be wary of when "Return of the Jedi" debuted.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.120
In footage from a 1983 newscast&nbsp;
recorded seemingly on opening night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.480
an audience member spills that&nbsp;
Darth Vader shows his face to Luke.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:31.120
“The big surprise was seeing Darth Vader unveil&nbsp;
himself to his son. It was very exciting.”

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.960
For the entirety of the series until this point,&nbsp;
Darth Vader had very much been masked and cloaked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:38.960
with no intention or even mention of&nbsp;
ever taking off his signature attire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:42.800
News that he appears in "Return of the Jedi" in&nbsp;
some sort of visually revealing way would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.440
been a major spoiler. Elsewhere in the same set of&nbsp;
interviews, another audience member reveals that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.440
the movie ends in celebration. While this perhaps&nbsp;
could have been predicted for those who knew the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:55.920
movie was the finale to a trilogy, even a happy&nbsp;
ending wasn't necessarily guaranteed and still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:59.840
would have at least been a minor spoiler.&nbsp;
Watch out, viewers of the nightly news!

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.720
Perhaps unsurprisingly — given the extreme&nbsp;
popularity for all things "Star Wars" from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:10.160
series' inception in 1977 — "Return of the Jedi"&nbsp;
did wonders at the box office and made history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:14.080
The New York Times excitedly broke the news a&nbsp;
few days later that the movie's opening day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:20.160
May 25, 1983, earned $6.2 million, the biggest&nbsp;
opening day in movie history at that time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.320
It beat the previous record held by&nbsp;
1982's "Star Trek II: The Wrath of Khan."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.240
Despite the phenomenon that "Star Wars"&nbsp;
clearly was, the Times noted the success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.440
of "Return of the Jedi" was furthermore&nbsp;
impressive because its release day was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.440
Wednesday rather than the traditional Friday&nbsp;
opening. Even on a weeknight, nothing was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.440
going to keep audiences away from seeing what&nbsp;
happened next for Luke Skywalker or Han Solo.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:42.000
“Over my dead body.”

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.760
In keeping with tradition, future "Star&nbsp;
Wars" films would achieve this same feat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:49.520
When perusing Box Office Mojo's list&nbsp;
of biggest opening day box offices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.480
we can see other movies in the series&nbsp;
after "Return of the Jedi" broke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.720
opening day records at the time of their&nbsp;
respective releases, including 1999's "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:01.440
Phantom Menace," 2005's "Revenge of the&nbsp;
Sith," and 2015's "The Force Awakens."

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.760
It can be difficult to please everyone when&nbsp;
it comes to sequels, especially if it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.400
last entry in a series. This is especially evident&nbsp;
when considering the contradictory opinions from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.560
critics upon the release of “Return of the Jedi”.&nbsp;
For instance, The Hollywood Reporter lamented:

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:18.560
“To be sure, the film abounds in action.&nbsp;
Some new peril besets Luke Skywalker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.160
Han Solo or Princess Leia almost&nbsp;
too regularly every 10 minutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:25.840
But there’s a kind of desperation about it,&nbsp;
a feeling that Lucas and co-writer Lawrence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.680
Kasdan are simply trying to figure out what&nbsp;
they can do next to amuse the kiddies.”

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.600
By contrast, the Washington Post couldn’t&nbsp;
sing the film’s praises enough, saying :

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.000
“"Return Of the Jedi," a feat of mass enchantment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.320
puts the happy finishing touches on George&nbsp;
Lucas' "Star Wars" saga. It was worth the wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.080
and the work is now an imposing landmark&nbsp;
in contemporary popular culture.”

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:47.680
It’s all water under the bridge now, of&nbsp;
course, but it’s nonetheless fascinating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.200
to wonder how these differing perspectives&nbsp;
affected the expectations of those who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.400
yet to see the trilogy’s conclusion&nbsp;
in theaters for the very first time.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:58.800
Outside of broad opinions of the film, it’s&nbsp;
also interesting to take into account how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.640
critics were faced with teasing details from&nbsp;
“Return of the Jedi” without saying too much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:06.640
How does one describe Jabba the Hutt to&nbsp;
someone who hasn't seen the movie yet?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.320
Critic Roger Ebert summed up the infamous&nbsp;
intergalactic crime lord as such:

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.520
"[Jabba] is a slimy, gruesome, reptilian monster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.600
made of warts and teeth [...] a cross&nbsp;
between a toad and the Cheshire Cat."

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:19.280
Looking back from our current perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:23.200
we’d say that’s quite a visceral description&nbsp;
that still leaves a lot to the imagination!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:26.800
The British Film Institute’s description&nbsp;
poetically plays off of Ebert’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:32.960
labeling Jabba as, quote, “imposingly repellent”&nbsp;
and “a Toad inflated to nightmarish complacency.”

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:36.000
On the other hand, The Hollywood&nbsp;
Reporter kept things relatively vague,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.200
describing Jabba as, quote,&nbsp;
"truly frightening" and "gross."

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:43.680
Regardless of which review fans happened to&nbsp;
stumble upon in the lead-up to their initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:48.720
viewing experience, it goes without saying that&nbsp;
no collection of words can quite do Jabba justice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.840
You just have to see the sadistic,&nbsp;
oversized slug for yourself.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:59.040
[Eating noises]

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:02.320
In 1983, Jabba the Hutt and the&nbsp;
denizens of his secluded desert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:05.760
palace weren’t the only cosmic&nbsp;
creatures on moviegoers' minds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:10.320
Just one year prior, Steven Spielberg's "E.T.&nbsp;
the Extra-Terrestrial" had become a huge hit.

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:15.840
“E.T. home phone.”

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.040
While "E.T." and "Return of&nbsp;
the Jedi" are quite different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:22.320
audiences might have felt a similar&nbsp;
euphoria while viewing both movies.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:26.880
Starlog Magazine highlighted reader&nbsp;
reviews shortly after "Jedi" debuted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:29.600
Brian Richards from Minnesota&nbsp;
wrote to the publication:

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:33.760
“[This] is the first film since I saw 'E.T.'&nbsp;
that made me feel real good inside. I left the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:37.600
theater with my spirits soaring [...] 'Return&nbsp;
of the Jedi' is destined to become a classic!"

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:42.720
Other readers were not so enthused by the&nbsp;
trilogy’s conclusion, and mockingly compared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:47.840
it to, shall we say, less serious monuments of&nbsp;
pop culture. Frank Gerard from New Jersey wrote:

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:50.400
"'Return of the Jedi' was an&nbsp;
overwhelming disappointment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:53.840
I simply cannot believe George Lucas&nbsp;
has given to eager 'Star Wars' fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:57.680
everywhere a film which belongs on 'The&nbsp;
Muppet Show' or Saturday morning TV."

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:02.320
In the end though, most fans were simply blown&nbsp;
away. Barb Trimble from Mississippi gushed:

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:07.360
"This movie is a non-stop ride on that 'Star Wars'&nbsp;
rollercoaster. You barely have time to settle back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:11.840
in your seats after a 'white-knuckler' of a scene,&nbsp;
then it's off again for round two. Bravo, George!"

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:15.600
While it's true that "Return of the Jedi"&nbsp;
was the final movie in the original "Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:19.600
Wars" trilogy, there was plenty more&nbsp;
yet to come, and audiences knew this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:23.760
Even in 1983, it was public knowledge that&nbsp;
George Lucas intended to make nine "Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:27.760
Wars" movies and that "Return of the Jedi"&nbsp;
was placed as sixth in the story. Quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:32.240
the ambitious undertaking to reveal to the&nbsp;
general public! Nonetheless, it would be 16&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:36.960
years before the story expanded when 1999's "The&nbsp;
Phantom Menace" kicked off the prequel trilogy.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:40.080
“There’s always a bigger fish.”

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:44.720
While 1983 moviegoers might have known more "Star&nbsp;
Wars" was on the way, they didn't expect Luke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:50.480
Leia, or Han Solo to ever re-enter the narrative,&nbsp;
thanks to Roger Ebert’s 1983 review. He said:

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:54.960
“If George Lucas persists in his plan to make nine&nbsp;
Star Wars movies, this will nevertheless be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:59.520
last we'll see of Luke, Han and Leia, although&nbsp;
the robots will be present in all the films.”

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.880
The droids would indeed continue to&nbsp;
make appearances, but so would the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:06.800
three original leads, even if it took a bit&nbsp;
longer for them to return to the series.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.640
"Return of the Jedi" was more than&nbsp;
just a movie to some of its viewers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:13.440
Inspired by its characters,&nbsp;
story, and filmmaking technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:18.240
some audience members in 1983 left the theater&nbsp;
feeling inspired to make movies of their own.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:23.200
This included Brad Bird, who at the time was 25&nbsp;
years old and working as an animator at Disney.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:27.120
Bird would later direct "The Iron Giant"&nbsp;
and "The Incredibles," among other films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:31.520
In the foreword of the 2013 edition of the book&nbsp;
"The Making of Return of the Jedi," Bird shares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:35.120
that he snuck into a snuck into a Lucasfilm crew&nbsp;
screening of "Return of the Jedi" and marveled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:40.320
at seeing the film in the same room as George&nbsp;
Lucas and director Richard Marquand. Bird writes:

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:42.560
"I was now surrounded by the very geniuses who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:46.640
brought this rarest of cinematic sagas to&nbsp;
a satisfying conclusion in grand style.”

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:51.200
However, Bird wasn't wild about everything in&nbsp;
the movie, specifically feeling like Luke's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:54.160
character had made an unrealistic&nbsp;
leap from the hero-in-training he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:57.440
was at the end of "The Empire Strikes&nbsp;
Back" to the confident protagonist he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:01.120
suddenly appeared to be by the beginning&nbsp;
of "Return of the Jedi." Bird reflects:

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:04.000
"The point is not that I, or any other fan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:08.480
agree with every single choice George has made in&nbsp;
telling his epic saga. The point is that we care."

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:13.360
There are, shall we say, polarizing creative&nbsp;
choices within the "Star Wars" universe that fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:17.200
have different perspectives on. While today, it's&nbsp;
easy to look back on films that are considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:21.760
classics and assume that nothing was controversial&nbsp;
at the time of their release, "Return of the Jedi"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:25.760
actually put fans at odds when it came to&nbsp;
a new creature the film introduced: Ewoks.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:31.840
“Point that thing someplace else.”

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:32.800
[Ewokese]

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:36.160
The cuddly, bear-like animals on the&nbsp;
moon of Endor turn out to be quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:40.560
the soldiers when faced with threats. In&nbsp;
1983, audiences were divided on what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:43.920
thought of Ewoks and how they welcomed&nbsp;
them – or didn't – into the series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:48.480
Some audience members criticized the Ewoks as&nbsp;
being nothing but a marketing device to sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:53.920
toys. The subject, it seems, was something of a&nbsp;
hot topic among fans in the '80s. Good thing we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.920 --> 00:11:00.200
moved on past those kinds of petty squabbles about&nbsp;
things that we claim ruin "Star Wars," right?

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:01.200
[laughter]

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:05.520
Just kidding. Ewoks are definitely&nbsp;
still a divisive subject to this day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:09.600
Love them or hate them, though, the Ewoks went&nbsp;
on to star in their own made-for-TV movies and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:14.240
animated TV series – and none of it would&nbsp;
have happened without "Return of the Jedi."

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:18.800
